  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Solve each of the following inequalities, by considering two cases: $x>0$ and $x<0$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{2}{x}<6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x<0$ or $x>\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\nCombine the solutions from the two sign cases.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&<6x\\\\x&>\\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&>6x\\\\x&<\\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nEvery $x<0$ satisfies the second case. Therefore, $x<0$ or $x>\\dfrac{1}{3}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{x}>3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<x<\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\nThe negative case has no solutions.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1&>3x\\\\x&<\\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $0<x<\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}1&<3x\\\\x&>\\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $x<0$. Therefore, $0<x<\\dfrac{1}{3}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{4}{x}\\le 8$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x<0$ or $x\\ge\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nCombine the solutions from the two sign cases.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4&\\le8x\\\\x&\\ge\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}4&\\ge8x\\\\x&\\le\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nEvery $x<0$ satisfies the second case. Therefore, $x<0$ or $x\\ge\\dfrac{1}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{3}{x}\\ge 6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<x\\le\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe negative case has no solutions.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&\\ge6x\\\\x&\\le\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $0<x\\le\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&\\le6x\\\\x&\\ge\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $x<0$. Therefore, $0<x\\le\\dfrac{1}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{2}{x}>-3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x<-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ or $x>0$.\n\nCombine the solutions from the two sign cases.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&>-3x\\\\x&>-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nEvery $x>0$ satisfies this case.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&<-3x\\\\x&<-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by $-3$ also reverses the inequality. Therefore, $x<-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ or $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{3}{x}<-6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-\\dfrac{1}{2}<x<0$.\n\nThe positive case has no solutions.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&<-6x\\\\x&<-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $x>0$.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&>-6x\\\\x&>-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by $-6$ also reverses the inequality. Combining with $x<0$ gives $-\\dfrac{1}{2}<x<0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 96
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{2}{x}\\le-3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\le x<0$.\n\nThe positive case has no solutions.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&\\le-3x\\\\x&\\le-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $x>0$.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&\\ge-3x\\\\x&\\ge-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by $-3$ also reverses the inequality. Combining with $x<0$ gives $-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\le x<0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 96
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q7-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{3}{x}\\ge\\dfrac{4}{5}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<x\\le\\dfrac{15}{4}$.\n\nThe negative case has no solutions.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&\\ge\\frac{4x}{5}\\\\15&\\ge4x\\\\x&\\le\\frac{15}{4}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $0<x\\le\\dfrac{15}{4}$.\n\nCase 2: $x<0$. Multiplying by $x$ reverses the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&\\le\\frac{4x}{5}\\\\15&\\le4x\\\\x&\\ge\\frac{15}{4}\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $x<0$. Therefore, $0<x\\le\\dfrac{15}{4}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 106
      }
    ]
  },
